// =============================================================================
// measures.dax
// Power BI semantic model measures for the Meridian Lead Exchange analysis.
//
// Create these in a dedicated measure table (Home > Enter Data > name it
// "_Measures", load one blank column, then hide that column). Keeping measures
// out of the fact table stops them from being mistaken for columns and keeps
// the field list readable as the model grows.
//
// Convention used throughout: measures reference other measures rather than
// repeating aggregation logic. Total Cost is defined once; CPA, ROI, and every
// variance measure consume it. Changing the cost definition then changes it
// everywhere, which is the whole reason to build a semantic model instead of
// twelve separate visuals with their own arithmetic.
// =============================================================================


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BASE MEASURES
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Total Leads =
COUNTROWS ( fct_leads )


Conversions =
SUM ( fct_leads[is_converted] )


Total Cost =
SUM ( fct_leads[acquisition_cost] )


Total Revenue =
SUM ( fct_leads[commission_revenue] )


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DERIVED PERFORMANCE MEASURES
//
// DIVIDE is used rather than the "/" operator throughout. DIVIDE returns BLANK
// on a zero denominator instead of an error, so a slicer selection that filters
// down to a segment with no conversions renders an empty card rather than
// breaking every visual on the page.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Conversion Rate =
DIVIDE ( [Conversions], [Total Leads] )
// Format as percentage in the Measure tools ribbon, 1 decimal place.


CPA =
DIVIDE ( [Total Cost], [Conversions] )
// Cost per acquisition. Blank when a segment has produced no conversions,
// which is correct: CPA is undefined, not infinite.


Net Contribution =
[Total Revenue] - [Total Cost]


ROI =
DIVIDE ( [Net Contribution], [Total Cost] )


Revenue Per Lead =
DIVIDE ( [Total Revenue], [Total Leads] )
// Useful alongside CPA because it is defined even for zero-conversion segments,
// so it degrades more gracefully in deep slicer selections.


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SHARE-OF-TOTAL MEASURES
//
// ALLSELECTED removes the filter from the current visual row while respecting
// slicers applied to the page. This is what makes a "% of spend" column total
// to 100% within the visual rather than against the entire dataset.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

% of Total Spend =
DIVIDE (
    [Total Cost],
    CALCULATE ( [Total Cost], ALLSELECTED ( fct_leads ) )
)


% of Total Conversions =
DIVIDE (
    [Conversions],
    CALCULATE ( [Conversions], ALLSELECTED ( fct_leads ) )
)


// Efficiency index. Above 1.0 means the segment delivers a greater share of
// conversions than the share of budget it consumes. This single measure is
// what makes the Display versus Affiliate story legible without a table.
Efficiency Index =
DIVIDE ( [% of Total Conversions], [% of Total Spend] )


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DATA QUALITY MEASURES
//
// Surfacing imputation rates on the dashboard itself is deliberate. A metric
// built partly on imputed inputs should say so where it is consumed, not only
// in documentation nobody opens.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Imputed Cost Records =
CALCULATE ( [Total Leads], fct_leads[is_cost_imputed] = TRUE )


% Cost Imputed =
DIVIDE ( [Imputed Cost Records], [Total Leads] )


Data Quality Flag =
IF (
    [% Cost Imputed] > 0.10,
    "Review: over 10% of cost values imputed",
    "OK"
)
// Drives conditional formatting on a dashboard card. Threshold is a judgement
// call, set here at the point where imputation starts to move segment CPA
// materially rather than at any statistical boundary.


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TIME INTELLIGENCE
//
// All of these require dim_date to be marked as a date table
// (Table tools > Mark as date table > date_key).
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Leads PW =
CALCULATE ( [Total Leads], DATEADD ( dim_date[date_key], -7, DAY ) )
// Prior week, for a like-for-like comparison on a weekly reporting cadence.


Leads WoW Change =
[Total Leads] - [Leads PW]


Leads WoW % =
DIVIDE ( [Leads WoW Change], [Leads PW] )


Net Contribution Running Total =
CALCULATE (
    [Net Contribution],
    FILTER (
        ALLSELECTED ( dim_date[date_key] ),
        dim_date[date_key] <= MAX ( dim_date[date_key] )
    )
)
// Cumulative margin burn across the period. Reads far better than daily net on
// a line chart, because it shows the compounding loss rather than daily noise.


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STATISTICAL CONFIDENCE
//
// Ports the Wilson score interval from the SQL layer into the semantic model so
// that confidence bounds respond to slicers. A conversion rate shown without
// its interval invites over-reading of small segments, which was the central
// analytical flaw in the original Excel version of this analysis.
//
//   centre = (p + z^2/2n) / (1 + z^2/n)
//   half   = z * sqrt( p(1-p)/n + z^2/4n^2 ) / (1 + z^2/n)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Conversion Rate CI Lower =
VAR n          = [Total Leads]
VAR p          = [Conversion Rate]
VAR z          = 1.96
VAR Denominator = 1 + DIVIDE ( z * z, n )
VAR Centre      = DIVIDE ( p + DIVIDE ( z * z, 2 * n ), Denominator )
VAR HalfWidth   =
    DIVIDE (
        z * SQRT ( DIVIDE ( p * ( 1 - p ), n ) + DIVIDE ( z * z, 4 * n * n ) ),
        Denominator
    )
RETURN
    IF ( n > 0, MAX ( Centre - HalfWidth, 0 ) )


Conversion Rate CI Upper =
VAR n          = [Total Leads]
VAR p          = [Conversion Rate]
VAR z          = 1.96
VAR Denominator = 1 + DIVIDE ( z * z, n )
VAR Centre      = DIVIDE ( p + DIVIDE ( z * z, 2 * n ), Denominator )
VAR HalfWidth   =
    DIVIDE (
        z * SQRT ( DIVIDE ( p * ( 1 - p ), n ) + DIVIDE ( z * z, 4 * n * n ) ),
        Denominator
    )
RETURN
    IF ( n > 0, MIN ( Centre + HalfWidth, 1 ) )


Evidence Strength =
VAR n           = [Total Leads]
VAR Successes   = [Conversions]
VAR IntervalWidth = [Conversion Rate CI Upper] - [Conversion Rate CI Lower]
RETURN
    SWITCH (
        TRUE (),
        n = 0,                              BLANK (),
        Successes < 5 || n < 50,            "Insufficient sample",
        IntervalWidth <= 0.12,              "Actionable",
        "Directional"
    )
// Mirrors the SQL classification exactly. Uses the conventional minimum of five
// observed successes for inference on a proportion, rather than interval width
// alone, because a segment with one conversion produces a deceptively narrow
// interval simply by sitting near zero.


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DYNAMIC TITLES
//
// Bind to a visual's title via Format > Title > fx > Field value. Makes an
// exported screenshot self-describing, which matters when a dashboard image
// gets pasted into a deck without its slicer context.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Selected Period Title =
"Marketplace Performance: "
    & FORMAT ( MIN ( dim_date[date_key] ), "d mmm yyyy" )
    & " to "
    & FORMAT ( MAX ( dim_date[date_key] ), "d mmm yyyy" )


Headline Summary =
VAR RoiText = FORMAT ( [ROI], "0.0%" )
RETURN
    IF (
        [ROI] < 0,
        "Portfolio operating at a loss: " & RoiText & " ROI on "
            & FORMAT ( [Total Cost], "$#,##0" ) & " of media spend",
        "Portfolio profitable: " & RoiText & " ROI on "
            & FORMAT ( [Total Cost], "$#,##0" ) & " of media spend"
    )
